  <section>
    <h2>When can you avoid electrical work?</h2>
    <p>Many conventional electric sauna heaters need a dedicated circuit. For example, the North American 6 kW and 8 kW Harvia Spirit models use a 240V supply and have specific wiring requirements in their <a href="https://resources.saunaplace.com/pdfs/Harvia-Spirit-IOM.pdf">installation manual</a>.</p>
    <p>The P1 uses pellets as its heat source and electricity to operate the stove. Its standard-outlet design may save electrical work where a suitable outlet already exists. If you need to add or relocate an outlet, or the existing circuit is unsuitable, you may still need an electrician.</p>
    <p>Before relying on an existing outlet, check the P1 installation requirements and the requirements for your location. A plug that fits is not enough to establish that the supply is suitable.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Make room for both sides of the stove</h2>
    <p>The P1 installs through the sauna wall. The firebox and stones are inside; the hopper is outside. Plan for the wall opening, room around the heater, and space outside to load pellets and service the stove.</p>
    <p>The published specifications list a sauna volume of 210–500 cubic feet and wall thicknesses of 1.5–6.5 inches. Measure your room and wall before choosing a location. Insulation and glazing also affect heating needs, so room volume alone does not establish suitability.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Plan the exhaust and clearances</h2>
    <p>A pellet stove needs an exhaust route outdoors. The location must also allow the required clearances from combustible materials, floor protection, and air supply. The sauna room needs its own ventilation plan.</p>
    <p>These are part of the installation cost even if no new electrical circuit is needed. Compare the complete job: stove, venting, wall work, protection, and labor. A simple electrical connection does not necessarily mean a simpler or cheaper installation overall.</p>
    <p>The P1 is available for pre-order, and its <a href="/p1-pellet-sauna-stove#specifications">specifications currently list certification as pending</a>. Confirm the final installation instructions and local requirements before making structural changes or ordering vent components.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What to send us about your sauna</h2>
    <p>If you’re considering a P1, a few details will help us discuss the fit:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Interior length, width, and height, plus details of insulation and glass areas.</li>
      <li>Wall thickness and photos of the proposed location, inside and outside.</li>
      <li>The location of the existing outlet and what you know about its circuit.</li>
      <li>A possible exhaust route and space to reach the exterior hopper.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><a href="/#contact">Send us your project details</a> before committing to a heater location. A backyard sauna or cabin may be a good candidate, but the fit depends on the actual room and installation.</p>
  </section>
